// counters-demo — a tour of the LaTeX-style named counters package.
//
// The counters package gives a document the same machinery LaTeX uses for every
// numbered thing: sections, figures, equations, footnotes. A counter is a named
// integer in a global store; you declare it, step it, set it, read it, and — the
// interesting part — chain one counter under another so the child restarts at one
// whenever the parent advances. The display formatters (arabic, roman, Roman,
// alph, Alph, fnsymbol) turn a counter's value into printable text.
//
// We layer this demo over the document package for its title block, numbered
// section headings and paragraph indentation. The document package already uses
// the counters package internally for its own section / figure numbering, so we
// take care to declare only fresh counter names (step, item, note, part, chap,
// fig2, sec2) that cannot collide with the ones document reserves.

\use{document}
\use{counters}

\title {The counters Package}
\author {A texish demonstration}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A walk through every public command of the counters package: declaring a counter
with \verb|\newcounter|, advancing it with \verb|\stepcounter|, assigning and
adjusting it with \verb|\setcounter| and \verb|\addtocounter|, reading it with
\verb|\value|, rendering it through the six display formatters, and — the heart
of the package — binding a child counter under a parent with \verb|\counterwithin|
so it resets automatically.
\end{abstract}

\section {Declaring and stepping a counter}

A counter is created with \verb|\newcounter|, which registers the name in the
global store with an initial value of zero. We declare one called \verb|step|
here. Reading it back with \verb|\value| and formatting with \verb|\arabic| shows
the starting value:

\newcounter{step}

\begin{quote}
\noindent After \verb|\newcounter{step}|, \verb|step| is \arabic{\value{step}}.
\end{quote}

// \stepcounter adds one and also runs the reset list (which does nothing here,
// since step has no children yet).
The command \verb|\stepcounter| advances a counter by one. Stepping \verb|step|
three times in a row gives the running values \stepcounter{step}\arabic{\value{step}},
\stepcounter{step}\arabic{\value{step}} and \stepcounter{step}\arabic{\value{step}}.
The counter now holds \arabic{\value{step}}.

\section {Setting, adding, and reading}

\verb|\setcounter| assigns an exact value, ignoring whatever the counter held
before. \verb|\addtocounter| adds a (possibly negative) amount to the current
value. \verb|\value| reads the counter back so it can be formatted or used in
arithmetic.

// Force step to a known value, then push it around to show both operations.
We set \verb|step| to 40 with \verb|\setcounter{step}{40}|\setcounter{step}{40},
add 2 with \verb|\addtocounter{step}{2}|\addtocounter{step}{2}, giving
\arabic{\value{step}}, and then subtract 5 with \verb|\addtocounter{step}{-5}|\addtocounter{step}{-5},
leaving \arabic{\value{step}}.

\section {The six display formatters}

A counter's value is just an integer; the formatters decide how it looks on the
page. Each takes a number — here the value of a counter — and emits its text. We
park a counter named \verb|item| at the value 4 and show all six formats applied
to the same number:

\newcounter{item}
\setcounter{item}{4}
\begin{quote}
\noindent\verb|\arabic|: \arabic{\value{item}}

\noindent\verb|\roman|: \roman{\value{item}}

\noindent\verb|\Roman|: \Roman{\value{item}}

\noindent\verb|\alph|: \alph{\value{item}}

\noindent\verb|\Alph|: \Alph{\value{item}}

\noindent\verb|\fnsymbol|: \fnsymbol{\value{item}}
\end{quote}

// fnsymbol cycles through *  †  ‡  §  ¶  ‖ and then doubles them. Walk a counter
// across the boundary to show the wrap to doubled symbols.
The footnote symbols follow LaTeX's classic order and double once they run past
six. Counting \verb|note| from 1 to 8 gives:
\newcounter{note}
\setcounter{note}{0}
\begin{quote}
\noindent
\stepcounter{note}1 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}2 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}3 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}4 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}5 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}6 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}7 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}\quad
\stepcounter{note}8 $\to$ \fnsymbol{\value{note}}
\end{quote}

\section {Automatic resets with counterwithin}

This is the package's central feature. \verb|\counterwithin{child}{parent}|
declares that whenever \verb|parent| is stepped, \verb|child| is reset to zero —
exactly the LaTeX reset list that makes a figure number restart at 1 in every new
chapter, or an equation number restart in every section.

// Worked example: chap (parent) and fig2 (child). fig2 resets each time chap steps.
We declare a parent counter \verb|chap| and a child \verb|fig2|, and bind the
child under the parent:

\begin{quote}
\noindent\verb|\newcounter{chap}  \newcounter{fig2}  \counterwithin{fig2}{chap}|
\end{quote}

\newcounter{chap}
\newcounter{fig2}
\counterwithin{fig2}{chap}

// Step chap, then fig2 a few times; step chap again and watch fig2 fall back to 1.
Now we step \verb|chap|, advance \verb|fig2| a few times within it, then step
\verb|chap| again. The figure number is printed as \verb|chap.fig2|, and you can
see it restart at 1 each time the chapter advances:

\setcounter{chap}{0}
\begin{quote}
\noindent
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}

\noindent
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}

\noindent
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}\quad
\stepcounter{fig2}Figure \arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{fig2}}
\end{quote}

\noindent The \verb|fig2| counter never has to be reset by hand: stepping
\verb|chap| does it, because \verb|\stepcounter| runs the reset list after it
advances the parent.

\section {Three-level nesting}

The reset is recursive: resetting a counter also resets every counter bound under
\emph{it}. So a three-level chain — a grandparent \verb|part|, a parent
\verb|chap|, and a grandchild \verb|sec2| — cascades. Stepping \verb|part| resets
\verb|chap| to zero, and resetting \verb|chap| in turn resets \verb|sec2|.

// Build the chain: sec2 within chap, chap within part. We reuse chap but rebind it
// under the new grandparent part, and reset all three to a clean state first.
\newcounter{part}
\newcounter{sec2}
\counterwithin{sec2}{chap}
\counterwithin{chap}{part}

\begin{quote}
\noindent\verb|\counterwithin{chap}{part}  \counterwithin{sec2}{chap}|
\end{quote}

\setcounter{part}{0}
\setcounter{chap}{0}
\setcounter{sec2}{0}

// Number reads part.chap.sec2. Step the grandparent twice, with inner steps
// between, to show both levels falling back to 1.
We number as \verb|part.chap.sec2|. Watch every level below the one we step fall
back to 1:

\begin{quote}
\noindent
\stepcounter{part}\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}\quad
\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}\quad
\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}\quad
\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}

\noindent
\stepcounter{part}\stepcounter{chap}\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}\quad
\stepcounter{sec2}\arabic{\value{part}}.\arabic{\value{chap}}.\arabic{\value{sec2}}
\end{quote}

\noindent When \verb|part| steps to 2, \verb|chap| falls from 2 back to 1 — its
own \verb|\stepcounter{chap}| then takes it to 1 — and \verb|sec2| falls back to 1
underneath, all from advancing the grandparent. The single \verb|\stepcounter{part}|
cascaded a reset through both levels below it.

\section {Mixed formats on one chain}

Because each level is formatted independently, a numbering scheme can mix styles.
Here the same \verb|part.chap.sec2| chain is rendered the way a book might: the
part as an uppercase roman numeral, the chapter as a letter, and the section as
an arabic number.

\begin{quote}
\noindent
Part \Roman{\value{part}}, Chapter \Alph{\value{chap}}, Section \arabic{\value{sec2}}:
this is part \Roman{\value{part}}-\Alph{\value{chap}}-\arabic{\value{sec2}}.
\end{quote}

\noindent The values are unchanged from the previous section; only the formatter
applied to each \verb|\value| differs.

\vfill\eject
